    "answer": {
      "short": "Step 2: squaring introduces extra candidates. Only $\\theta=\\frac\\pi4,\\frac{5\\pi}4$ satisfy the original equation.",
      "worked": "Step 2 is not reversible: $\\sin^2\\theta=\\cos^2\\theta$ allows both $\\sin\\theta=\\cos\\theta$ and $\\sin\\theta=-\\cos\\theta$.\n\nAt $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}4$ and $\\frac{7\\pi}4$, sine and cosine have opposite signs, so these candidates fail the original equation. At $\\theta=\\frac\\pi4$ and $\\frac{5\\pi}4$, the ratios are equal.\n\nThus the valid solutions are $\\theta=\\frac\\pi4,\\frac{5\\pi}4$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
